# The Borrow Contract Ledger of Nikaia's standard library.
#
# Part III 13.5 says a published package ships its ledger, so that a consumer
# builds against contracts rather than guesses. This is that file for `std`, and
# it is the one a program's compiler reads when the program calls `io::…` or
# `fs::…` - what a caller has to know about a function it cannot see the body
# of: whether it may pause (`sync`), whether it may fail and with what
# (`throws`), and what its result may point into (`returns`).
#
# `throws = ["?"]` on every entry here is not a placeholder but the honest
# answer: these functions fail with Rust's errors, which have no Nikaia type to
# name (ADR-023 D1, and ADR-024 D1's `?` - the absence of a claim). A caller
# reads it as "it fails", which is what the boolean before it said.
#
# **Two origins, and the difference is not hidden.** The entries for modules
# written in Nikaia are *inferred* by the Stage 0 compiler from their source,
# and `crates/nikaia/tests/contracts.rs` regenerates them and fails if this file
# has drifted. The entries for the modules that are still Rust cannot be
# inferred by a compiler that does not read Rust, so they are **written here and
# reviewed like code**. That is the honest shape of a library whose
# implementation is half in another language, and the share that is written by
# hand shrinks as more of `std` becomes Nikaia (ADR-014).
#
# An absent `sync` means *not* sync - which is the promise this file exists to
# make. An absent *entry* means nothing is known, and a caller may not assume.
#
# **Present, `sync` says which kind of promise it is** (ADR-027). `sync = true`
# is one the source made and the compiler checked; `sync = "inferred"` is one
# the body implies, derived from the calls it makes. A caller uses them
# identically - both mean "this cannot pause" - and a *diff* must not, because
# withdrawing a promise someone wrote is a decision and losing an inferred one
# is a consequence of an edit somewhere else. Every entry below that is written
# by hand is `true` by definition: a promise about a Rust body, made here.
#
# **A parameter whose Rust type is a bound rather than a type is recorded `?`.**
# `fs::write(path: impl AsRef<Path>, data: impl AsRef<[u8]>)` accepts a `String`
# and a `&str` and a buffer, so writing `&str` here would make the type checker
# reject `fs::write(path, text)` for a `String` - a correct program, refused.
# `?` is the absence of a claim (ADR-024 D1) and it is the true one: the arity
# is still checked, and the type is not claimed because there is no one type.
#
# `touches` says which resources a function reaches and whether it changes them
# (ADR-033, Part I 8.1.1). It is what lets two operations that meet on nothing
# run at the same time. **Absent means it touches everything**, which is the
# opposite of how an absent `throws` reads and is deliberate: an effect nobody
# described has to order against everything, or a missing contract would make a
# program wrong rather than merely slow. Only the entries below say it; every
# other function in this file keeps the order it was written in.
#
# The resources it may name are a **closed list** - `file(path)`, `stdin`,
# `stdout`, `stderr`, `args` - kept in `contracts::touch`. It is closed because
# two touches of different kinds never conflict, so a kind the compiler did not
# know would be disjoint from every kind there is: a typo here would buy an
# overlap rather than fail to buy one. A word from outside the list is answered
# the way ADR-033 D4 answers everything it cannot read - the function reaches
# everything and keeps its place - and the list grows when a function needs it
# (ADR-028 D5), never before.
#
# **`touches = []` is a claim about resources and not about memory.** A function
# that changes something it was handed - `Vec::push`, `String::push_str`,
# `HashMap::entry` - reaches no *resource*, and writing `touches = []` on it
# would still be true in ADR-033 D2's terms and wrong to write: the ordering
# analysis finds a data dependency by comparing what a statement binds against
# what the next one mentions, and `xs.push(a)` twice binds nothing and mentions
# `xs` in both. Memory is the borrow checker's question (ADR-033 D3) and this
# analysis does not have its answer, so the functions that change what they are
# given say nothing here and keep their order. The same goes for a function
# that runs a lambda - `Vec::sort_by_key`, `Entry::and_modify` - where a
# `touches = []` would be a claim about somebody else's code.
#
# **And `sync`'s answer to that does not transfer here.** `sync = "from(f)"`
# (ADR-029 D3) lets a higher-order function say "the lambda decides", sound
# because `sync` is a fact about a whole *function* and the lambda's body is
# walked as part of the function that writes it. `touches` is consumed per
# **statement**, and nothing walks a lambda for an effect - so "the lambda
# decides", read as "this call adds nothing", would buy an overlap on an
# incomplete touch set. There is no `touches = "from(f)"`, and the entry a
# higher-order function would need says *add* what the lambda reaches rather
# than assume it away (ADR-029 D3, and `contracts::order`'s `Expr::Closure`
# arm).
#
# `crosses` on a `[type."…"]` says a value of that type **may cross a thread**
# (ADR-005 §1 Group B, `NK25xx`). It is only ever written for a type whose parts
# are Rust: a Nikaia `struct` records its `fields` and the check walks those,
# structurally, which is what that record asks for. Its absence is "nobody said"
# and not "it may not" - and "nobody said" is not permission, so the compiler
# will not put such a value on a thread of its own choosing.
#
# `sharing` on a `[fn."…"]` says which of a function's `Shared` positions are one
# allocation and which reference count each of those classes gets (ADR-037 D7).
# No `std` entry has one. `Shared::deref` is the only entry that mentions the
# type, and it takes the handle as a **borrow** and hands back a view of what is
# inside - so it has no `Shared` position of its own to classify: a borrow
# duplicates nothing (ADR-040 D1). `a_std_entry_that_takes_a_shared_needs_a_second_look`
# in `crates/nikaia/tests/sharing.rs` is what makes somebody look on the day an
# entry takes a handle by value or hands one back: the analysis treats a described
# callee as accounted for, so an entry that could keep a handle and cross a thread
# with it is a decision and not a line.
#
# `provenance` marks a **source**: a function whose result is bytes that entered
# the program from outside, and who chose them (ADR-010 D2). Its absence is not
# "trusted" - it is "this is not a source", which is what almost every function
# is. Every source `std` has today is the operator's own: a file they named, a
# pipe they connected, the arguments they typed. The untrusted ones - `http`,
# `net`, `db` - arrive with the modules that have them.
version = 2
toolchain = "nikaia 0.1.0"
inference = "stage0-signatures+sync-bodies+throws-bodies+sharing-bodies"

# --- Written by hand: the modules that are still Rust ------------------------

# The arguments the program was started with. Read-only and read by anyone:
# two `cli::args()` calls hand back the same vector, so they meet on nothing
# and neither has to wait for the other. It is the most-refused callee in
# `examples/` - ten adjacent pairs named it - and it was refused for no better
# reason than that nobody had written this line.
[fn."cli::args"]
pub = true
# `sync` because there is nothing here to pause on: the arguments are a vector
# the process was started with, and reading them is memory. It had no `sync`
# line for want of somebody classifying it rather than because anybody decided
# it could pause - and an absent `sync` means *not* sync, which is what this
# file's own note above says. Found by ADR-055 §6 step 3: with `std`'s pausing
# entries awaited, `cli::args()` became `cli::args().await` and `Args` is not a
# future.
sync = true
touches = ["args read"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "() -> Args"

# The argument at a position, and **there may not be one** - which is the half
# the corpus writes every time: `cli::args().nth(1) ?? "default"` opens six of
# the example programs. Without this entry `nth` was a method nothing described,
# so the whole `??` stood on a value of no type; with it, the `String?` is what
# the coalescing operator is for (Part I 2.3, 3.5).
#
# `sync` and `touches` are `cli::args`' own, for its reason: reading the vector
# the process was started with is memory.
[fn."Args::nth"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = ["args read"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "(&Args, at: i64) -> String?"

[fn."eprint"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = ["stderr write"]
signature = "(text: ?)"

[fn."eprintln"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = ["stderr write"]
signature = "(text: ?)"

[fn."fs::map"]
pub = true
touches = ["file(path) read"]
throws = ["?"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "(path: ?) -> Mapped"

[fn."fs::Mapped::deref"]
pub = true
sync = true
returns = "borrows(self)"
signature = "(&Mapped) -> &str"

[fn."fs::read"]
pub = true
touches = ["file(path) read"]
throws = ["?"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "(path: ?) -> Vec[u8]"

[fn."fs::read_to_string"]
pub = true
touches = ["file(path) read"]
throws = ["?"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "(path: ?) -> String"

[fn."fs::write"]
pub = true
touches = ["file(path) write"]
throws = ["?"]
signature = "(path: ?, data: ?; append: bool = false, create: bool = true)"

[fn."print"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = ["stdout write"]
signature = "(text: ?)"

[fn."println"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = ["stdout write"]
signature = "(text: ?)"

[fn."html::escape"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
returns = "borrows(text)"
signature = "(text: &str) -> ?"

[fn."io::read"]
pub = true
throws = ["?"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "() -> Vec[u8]"

[fn."io::lines"]
pub = true
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "() -> Lines"

[fn."io::read_to_string"]
pub = true
throws = ["?"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "() -> String"

[fn."list::ListExt::map"]
pub = true
sync = "from(f)"
signature = "(?, f: fn(?)) -> ?"

# --- Written by hand: the collections and text the language below provides ---
#
# `Vec`, `HashMap`, `str` and `String` are Rust's, reached through Nikaia's
# spellings, and a compiler that does not read Rust cannot infer them. They are
# here because ADR-028 gave them a second job: the type checker resolves
# `xs.push(x)` only if it knows what `xs` is, and the `sync` inference (ADR-027)
# can only vouch for a method call the checker resolved. Every one of these was
# **observed being asked for** by a program in this repository; none is here
# speculatively, because a wrong entry does not merely fail to help - it makes
# the checker reject a correct program.
#
# Types are `?` wherever the honest answer is "no one type" (ADR-024 D1). What
# an entry does claim is its **arity**, which is checked - so the risk of being
# wrong here is real and the corpus is what holds it down.
#
# **A signature may name its receiver's type arguments** (ADR-031): `$V` in
# `HashMap::entry` is whatever the map holds, bound at the call site and
# substituted away. A variable that stays unbound becomes `?`, never a name.
# The rule for where one may appear is narrow and deliberate: **in what flows
# out** - a result, a lambda's parameter - and never in an argument. What flows
# out is a promise this file makes and is wrong on its own account; what flows
# in is a constraint on somebody's program, and Rust's collections take more
# than their type parameters suggest (`get` by anything the key borrows as), so
# a variable there would reject correct code.
#
# **A higher-order method says `sync = "from(f)"`.** Whether `and_modify` pauses
# depends on the lambda it is given, and the ledger can say "it depends" since
# ADR-029 D3: `from(f)` names the parameter that decides, and a caller reads it
# as "this call adds no pausing of its own". `sync = true` here would be a
# promise about somebody else's code, and before `from` existed the honest
# alternative was to say nothing and cost every caller its inferred `sync`.
# The bound is ADR-029 D4: `from` is for a lambda that runs *before the call
# returns*, because that is the only case whose calls the caller has already
# counted. `a_detached_lambda_may_not_use_from` holds every `from` entry in this
# file to it.
#
# It does not generalise to the other columns. `throws` needs nothing - a
# lambda's failures are already in the enclosing function's error set, by the
# same walk - and `touches` must not be read this way at all, for the reason the
# header's note on `touches = []` gives.

[fn."f64::abs"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(f64) -> f64"

[fn."f64::sqrt"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(f64) -> f64"

# ## Arithmetic that does not abort (ADR-043 D2, D3)
#
# An overflow aborts, always (D1). These are how a program says it means
# something else, and they are named rather than spelled with a sign: both are
# rare, both are meant to be visible in the line that does them, and a sign for
# one would have to be written with `&`, which is borrowing here.
#
# **For `i32` and `i64` and no other integer type.** Part I 2.2 names those two,
# and ADR-028 D5's rule is that an entry exists because a program asked for it:
# the compiler accepts `u32` and the rest, but the specification does not offer
# them, so entries for them would be speculation about a surface that is not
# promised. The day Part I 2.2 grows, these grow with it.
#
# **No `saturating_shl` or `saturating_shr`**, because Rust has neither - D3 says
# "the same with `saturating_` wherever Rust has them" and this is where that
# clause bites.
#
# `touches = []` on each: a pure computation reaches no resource and binds its
# result, so two of them in a row may be reordered, which is what the header's
# note above asks the claim to mean.

[fn."i32::wrapping_add"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i32, i32) -> i32"

[fn."i32::wrapping_sub"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i32, i32) -> i32"

[fn."i32::wrapping_mul"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i32, i32) -> i32"

[fn."i32::wrapping_div"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i32, i32) -> i32"

[fn."i32::wrapping_neg"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i32) -> i32"

[fn."i32::wrapping_abs"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i32) -> i32"

[fn."i32::wrapping_shl"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i32, places: ?) -> i32"

[fn."i32::wrapping_shr"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i32, places: ?) -> i32"

[fn."i32::saturating_add"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i32, i32) -> i32"

[fn."i32::saturating_sub"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i32, i32) -> i32"

[fn."i32::saturating_mul"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i32, i32) -> i32"

[fn."i32::saturating_div"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i32, i32) -> i32"

[fn."i32::saturating_neg"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i32) -> i32"

[fn."i32::saturating_abs"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i32) -> i32"

[fn."i64::wrapping_add"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i64, i64) -> i64"

[fn."i64::wrapping_sub"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i64, i64) -> i64"

[fn."i64::wrapping_mul"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i64, i64) -> i64"

[fn."i64::wrapping_div"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i64, i64) -> i64"

[fn."i64::wrapping_neg"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i64) -> i64"

[fn."i64::wrapping_abs"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i64) -> i64"

[fn."i64::wrapping_shl"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i64, places: ?) -> i64"

[fn."i64::wrapping_shr"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i64, places: ?) -> i64"

[fn."i64::saturating_add"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i64, i64) -> i64"

[fn."i64::saturating_sub"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i64, i64) -> i64"

[fn."i64::saturating_mul"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i64, i64) -> i64"

[fn."i64::saturating_div"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i64, i64) -> i64"

[fn."i64::saturating_neg"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i64) -> i64"

[fn."i64::saturating_abs"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i64) -> i64"

# **Truncation has a name**, and the name carries the destination
# ([ADR-043](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-043.md) D7). Since D4 a conversion
# that may not fit aborts, so this is the only way left to say "keep the low
# digits" - said rather than assumed, exactly as `wrapping_` is said.
#
# One entry per destination, because that is what a signature can be written for:
# `truncating_i32` hands back an `i32` whatever it was given, and a single name
# with a type that follows the call site is not something the ledger can say.
#
# The sources are the two a Nikaia program can have a narrowing conversion out
# of: the larger integer and a floating-point number. There is no
# `truncating_f64` - nothing narrows *to* one in a program written in the types
# Part I 2.2 offers - and there is no `truncating_usize` either, since ADR-048 D1:
# a length is an `i64`, the machine-width type left the surface a program can
# write, and nothing narrows out of a type nobody can hold.
#
# `touches = []` and `sync = true` for the reason the arithmetic entries give: a
# pure computation that reaches no resource.

[fn."i64::truncating_i32"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(i64) -> i32"

[fn."f64::truncating_i32"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(f64) -> i32"

[fn."f64::truncating_i64"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(f64) -> i64"

[fn."HashMap::entry"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&HashMap[$K, $V], key: ?) -> Entry[$V]"

# `key: ?` and not `key: $K`, deliberately. Rust's `get` takes anything the key
# borrows as, so `m.get("x")` on a `HashMap[String, …]` is correct and `$K`
# would reject it. The rule is ADR-031's: a variable says what flows *out*, and
# `?` stays for what flows *in*.
# **A key may not be there**, which is what `-> $V` used to deny.
#
# It was not a thin entry but a **wrong** one, and the difference shows in what
# it cost: with `get` claiming a `$V`, `counts.get(k)?.n` - the honest Nikaia
# spelling - was refused as `NK1121` *"reaches through a `Tally`, which cannot
# be absent"*, while `counts.get(k).map_or(0, …)` compiled by writing Rust's
# `Option` surface through a hole. So the ledger refused the correct program and
# accepted the one this language has its own words for, which is Part III C.4's
# rule standing on its head.
[fn."HashMap::get"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&HashMap[$K, $V], key: ?) -> $V?"

[fn."HashMap::keys"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&HashMap[?, ?]) -> ?"

[fn."HashMap::len"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&HashMap[?, ?]) -> i64"

[fn."HashMap::new"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "() -> HashMap[?, ?]"

[fn."HashMap::values"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&HashMap[?, ?]) -> ?"

# Runs the lambda it is given, so what it does is what the lambda does - which
# is what `from(f)` says (ADR-029). Sound because the lambda runs *during* the
# call: its body belongs to the function that writes it, and its calls are
# counted there. A method that **stored** its lambda could not say this.
[fn."Entry::and_modify"]
pub = true
sync = "from(f)"
signature = "(Entry[$V], f: fn(&$V)) -> Entry[$V]"

[fn."Entry::or_insert"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(Entry[$V], value: ?) -> &$V"

# Also runs its lambda, before it returns.
[fn."Entry::or_insert_with"]
pub = true
sync = "from(f)"
signature = "(Entry[$V], f: fn()) -> &$V"

# `Shared[T]` is the type several parts of a program own at once (Part I 6.2).
# It is here rather than inferred for the reason the whole of this section is
# here: what it expands to is Rust - a reference count chosen per value
# (ADR-037 D7), the atomic one where the value may reach another thread and the
# plain one where `contracts::sharing` proves it cannot. At
# `user_parallelism = no` every one of them is plain, because nothing can cross
# there (ADR-061 D2).
#
# **There is no constructor entry, and there must not be one.** The annotation
# *is* the constructor: `let db: Shared[Connection] = connect(…)` makes the
# first handle, and a `Shared::new` would write the same thing twice
# (ADR-040 §3, Part I 6.2). There is no entry for making a second handle either,
# because a handle handed on by value is duplicated and there is nothing to call
# (ADR-040 D1).
#
# `deref` is the one entry, and it is what makes `serve(&db)` mean something.
# ADR-042 D2 sees a container through to what it holds wherever its ledger
# records a `deref`, and D2's own note says `Shared` is seen through "the moment
# it has a `deref` entry, with no further decision". This is that entry. `$T` is
# bound by the receiver (ADR-031), which is why one line answers for every
# `Shared` a program writes rather than for one instantiation.
[fn."Shared::deref"]
pub = true
sync = true
returns = "borrows(self)"
signature = "(&Shared[$T]) -> &$T"

# `String::new()` reaches nothing and hands back an empty one. Four adjacent
# pairs in `examples/` named it as the callee nobody had described, which is
# what asked for the entry.
[fn."String::new"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "() -> String"

[fn."String::chars"]
pub = true
sync = true
returns = "borrows(self)"
signature = "(&String) -> ?"

[fn."String::len"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&String) -> i64"

[fn."String::push"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&String, c: char)"

[fn."String::push_str"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&String, text: ?)"

[fn."Vec::is_empty"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&Vec[?]) -> bool"

[fn."Vec::len"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&Vec[?]) -> i64"

[fn."Vec::new"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "() -> Vec[?]"

[fn."Vec::push"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&Vec[$T], value: ?)"

[fn."Vec::sort"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&Vec[?])"

# Part II 12.2's four doors ([ADR-059](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-059.md)).
# Written down so the checker can resolve them: without an entry a call is "a
# method nothing describes", which costs the value its plain shape - and 12.2's
# own idiom cannot be `sync`, which `docs/mutex-floor.md` §3.1 found before these
# existed.
#
# `sync = "from(f)"` (ADR-029 D3) on the two that take a lambda: the lambda
# decides, which is the whole of ADR-005 D6's rule - the door itself never
# pauses, and what runs inside it is the caller's own code. Waiting to acquire is
# not pausing: the rule is about a suspension point while a lock is *held*, and
# the panic hook must be `sync` and may block (Part I 7.2).
#
# `get` and `set` carry no `sync` line and need none, for a stronger reason than
# the other two have: while the lock is open in either, no code of the user's
# runs at all (ADR-039 D10).
[fn."Locked::get"]
pub = true
touches = ["lock read"]
sync = true
signature = "(&Locked[$T]) -> $T"

[fn."Locked::set"]
pub = true
touches = ["lock write"]
sync = true
signature = "(&Locked[$T], value: $T)"

# **Reads in place and may not change what it is handed** (ADR-059 D1): the one
# door that answers a question about a large value without copying it.
[fn."Locked::access"]
pub = true
touches = ["lock read"]
sync = "from(f)"
signature = "(&Locked[$T], f: fn(&$T)) -> ?"

# **Where locked data changes** (D2). The old value goes in by value and the new
# one comes back, so no handle into the inside ever exists - and `old` is an
# ordinary immutable parameter, which is why no lambda in this language is handed
# something it may change.
[fn."Locked::update"]
pub = true
touches = ["lock write"]
sync = "from(f)"
# `fn($T)` and not `fn($T) -> $T`: this ledger's type language has no result on a
# lambda, which is a limit of the ledger rather than of the door. What it is read
# for here is the arity and the `sync` column.
signature = "(&Locked[$T], f: fn($T))"

# `SharedMut[T]` is the shared mutable type (Part I 6.2, ADR-039 D9): a value
# several parts own at once and any of them may change. **It is one name here and
# two hulls below** (ADR-064 D1) - the count and the lock - and the name is what
# the checker, this ledger and every message carry; only the emitter expands it.
#
# **The four doors are the same four**, so these entries are `Locked`'s with one
# name changed. They are here rather than reached through a `deref` because there
# is no `&` straight through a lock (Part I 6.2): you get in by opening it, and
# then what the lambda sees is an ordinary view.
#
# There is no constructor entry for the same reason there is none for `Locked` or
# `Shared`: this ledger's type language binds `$T` from the **receiver** (ADR-031),
# and a constructor has none - the type argument would have to come from what is
# handed in. So `SharedMut(0)` is the compiler's to type, the way `T?`'s `Some(…)`
# is (ADR-064 D2).
[fn."SharedMut::get"]
pub = true
touches = ["lock read"]
sync = true
signature = "(&SharedMut[$T]) -> $T"

[fn."SharedMut::set"]
pub = true
touches = ["lock write"]
sync = true
signature = "(&SharedMut[$T], value: $T)"

[fn."SharedMut::access"]
pub = true
touches = ["lock read"]
sync = "from(f)"
signature = "(&SharedMut[$T], f: fn(&$T)) -> ?"

[fn."SharedMut::update"]
pub = true
touches = ["lock write"]
sync = "from(f)"
signature = "(&SharedMut[$T], f: fn($T))"

# **The two doors over several locks** (Part II 12.3, ADR-065). They take the
# locks and then the block, and the acquisition order is by address so that two
# tasks naming the same two locks the other way round still take them alike.
#
# **No `signature`, and that is the decision rather than a gap** (ADR-065 D1).
# This ledger's type language binds `$T` from a receiver (ADR-031); a door over
# several locks has none, the locks hold different types, and how many there are
# is open. So the compiler types these itself, the way it types `SharedMut(0)`.
# What the ledger *can* say is the `sync` column, and that is the half a caller
# needs: an acquisition adds no pausing of its own, so the block decides - the
# same shape `access` has, for the same reason (ADR-029 D3).
[fn."access_all"]
pub = true
touches = ["lock read"]
sync = "from(f)"

[fn."update_all"]
pub = true
touches = ["lock write"]
sync = "from(f)"

[fn."Vec::sort_by_key"]
pub = true
sync = "from(f)"
signature = "(&Vec[$T], f: fn(&$T)) -> ?"

[fn."str::chars"]
pub = true
sync = true
returns = "borrows(self)"
signature = "(&str) -> ?"

[fn."str::len"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&str) -> i64"

# `n` is the `i64` a program can hold, not the `usize` Rust counts in
# (ADR-054 D2). The conversion is emitted at the call and a negative count
# aborts saying so - the parameter direction of ADR-048 D1, which that record
# left open and named this entry as the one site it had.
[fn."str::repeat"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&str, n: i64) -> String"

[fn."str::starts_with"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&str, prefix: ?) -> bool"

[fn."str::to_string"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(&str) -> String"

[fn."str::trim"]
pub = true
sync = true
returns = "borrows(self)"
signature = "(&str) -> &str"

# --- Inferred from `src/*.nika` ----------------------------------------------

# `touches = []` is a **claim** and not a blank: it reaches nothing, so it orders
# against nothing (ADR-033 D4). It is written here because this entry is checked
# against the `.nika` source that produces it, and `touch::infer` reads that body
# and answers the same ([ADR-067](adr-067) D2) - the two must agree.
[fn."text::digit_value"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(c: char) -> i32"

# What the program was started with, and the one type here that says it **may
# cross a thread** (`crosses`, ADR-005 §1 Group B). Its parts are Rust, so
# `contracts::send` cannot walk them and would answer "nobody said" - which is
# not permission, and would have cost `cli::args` the overlapping that the
# `touches` line above bought it (ADR-033 §8.1). The claim is a promise about a
# Rust type, written here and reviewed like code, exactly as `sync = true` is.
[type."cli::Args"]
pub = true
crosses = true

[type."fs::Mapped"]
pub = true

# A step of this one can **fail**, and that is a fact a caller cannot get from
# anywhere else: a `for` over it is a place the enclosing function can fail
# from, and the compiler makes that function say `throws` (ADR-025 D1, `NK2701`).
[type."io::Lines"]
pub = true
iterates = "throws"

# The type several parts of a program own at once (Part I 6.2), and the one type
# here whose `deref` entry above a caller is not meant to notice: a function that
# takes `&Connection` accepts a `&Shared[Connection]`, which is ADR-042 D2's
# rescue rather than a rule of this file's own.
#
# No `crosses` line, deliberately, and its absence is not "nobody said" here.
# `contracts::send` keeps `Shared` in its **container** list since ADR-037 D6, so
# a `Shared[T]` is answered by what `T` is: `Shared[String]` crosses and
# `Shared[Locked[i32]]` is undecided, for the lock's sake and not the count's. A
# `crosses = true` written here would answer for both, which is the one thing
# that is not true of the type.
# **What a `spawn` hands back** (Part I 8.2,
# [ADR-055](../../docs/specification/adr/adr-055.md) D5).
#
# No `crosses`, and here its absence really is "nobody said": whether a handle
# may cross a thread is a question about the value the task produces and about
# the executor that owns it, and the multi-threaded executor is not built
# (ADR-055 §6 step 1's `yes` half). An undecided crossing is handed on rather
# than read as permission (ADR-010 D1), which is what this file's own note
# above says.
[type."TaskHandle"]
pub = true

# `.join()` is where two tasks meet again, and it is a **suspension point**: the
# task fills a slot and wakes whoever is waiting on it, so joining gives the
# thread up rather than holding it. Hence no `sync` line, which is this file's
# way of saying it can pause - and the emitter turns that into the `.await`
# (ADR-055 D2).
#
# `$T` is bound by the receiver (ADR-031), so one line answers for every
# `TaskHandle` a program writes. It takes `self` by value because a value can
# only be joined once.
#
# No `throws`: a task's failure is the task's, and what `join` hands back is
# whatever the body's last statement was. A task whose body can fail hands back
# a value that says so, which is the same shape any other function has.
[fn."TaskHandle::join"]
pub = true
signature = "(TaskHandle[$T]) -> $T"

[type."Shared"]
pub = true
